Aram A. Avakian (April 23, 1926 – January 17, 1987) was an Armenian-American film editor and director. His work in the latter role includes "Jazz on a Summer's Day" (1960) and the indie film "End of the Road" (1970).
Aram Avagyan
Aram Avagyan (Armenian: Արամ Ավագյան , born 18 January 1991) is an Armenian amateur bantamweight boxer who won bronze medals at the European championships in 2013 and 2015. He was eliminated in the second bout at the 2016 Olympics. Avagyan studies criminology at the Russian Armenian University in Yerevan.
Aram Talalyan
Aram Talalyan (Armenian: Արամ Թալալյան ) is an Armenian cellist and conductor.
Avakian
Avakian (Armenian: Ավագյան ) (also spelled Avagian, Avagyan, Avakyan, or Avak'yan) is a common Armenian surname. It derives from the Armenian word "avag", meaning "oldest", "old", or "big", perhaps signifying that a male ancestor was a prominent individual in his community. It may also mean "son of Avak".
Aram Bakshian
Aram Bakshian Jr. (born 1944 ) is a native of Washington, DC. His heritage is Armenian. He has been a lifelong “wordsmith” plying his trade initially as an aide to Congressman William “Bill” Brock (1966-70), then as a special assistant and speechwriter for Republican National Committee (RNC) Chairman Senator Bob Dole (1971). His work at the RNC caught the eye of many within the White House. He joined the speechwriting staff of President Richard Nixon and, later, of President Ford (1972-75). He then became a senior consultant to Treasury Secretary William E. Simon (1976-77). Following his government service Aram went on the lecture circuit as well as becoming a senior fellow at the John F. Kennedy School of Government at Harvard before being brought back for White House service.

